## Overview

`service-opentelemetry` provides two focused modules:

- `services/opentelemetry/api.ts` configures server-side OTLP/HTTP JSON export and same-origin proxy routes for browser traces, metrics, and logs.
- `services/opentelemetry/browser.ts` configures browser OTLP export and captures global errors and unhandled promise rejections as failed spans.

The browser exporter posts traces to `/api/otel/v1/traces` by default. Browser metrics and logs are opt-in so idle pages do not continuously emit metric payloads or forward application logs. The API proxy forwards enabled signals to `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`, appending the standard `/v1/traces`, `/v1/metrics`, or `/v1/logs` path. Each route is enabled when the corresponding `OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER`, `OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER`, or `OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER` value includes `otlp`, avoiding browser CORS and collector exposure. The server exporter also reads `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` through Effect's `Otlp.layerFromConfig()`.

## Server Setup

Merge the application-owned routes, then inject `HttpApiOtlp.layer`. It provides the server OTLP exporter, browser proxy routes, HTTP server services, and tracer suppression for OTLP ingestion:

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";

import { HttpApiOtlp } from "@/services/opentelemetry/api";

const appRoutes = Layer.mergeAll(apiLayer, docsLayer, mcpLayer);
const appLayer = appRoutes.pipe(
  Layer.provideMerge(HttpApiOtlp.layer),
);
```

## Browser Setup

Install the configured browser layer globally on the Atom runtime used by the application API client. Runtime-level installation is required so browser-only spans are exported as well as propagated to API requests:

```ts
import { Atom } from "effect/unstable/reactivity";

import { BrowserOtlp } from "@/services/opentelemetry/browser";

const apiRuntime = Atom.context();

if (!import.meta.env.SSR) {
  apiRuntime.addGlobalLayer(
    BrowserOtlp.layer({ serviceName: "my-app-web" }),
  );
}

export class ApiClient extends AtomHttpApi.Service<ApiClient>()("ApiClient", {
  api: Api,
  httpClient: FetchHttpClient.layer,
  baseUrl: import.meta.env.VITE_SITE_URL,
  runtime: apiRuntime,
}) {}
```

`BrowserOtlp.layer` accepts optional `serviceVersion`, resource `attributes`, `baseUrl`, and shared `headers`. Traces are enabled by default and batch every 30 seconds; browser metrics and logs are disabled by default. Enable them with `metrics: {}` or `logs: {}`. Signal objects accept their Effect exporter options directly, for example `traces: { exportInterval: "15 seconds", maxBatchSize: 250 }` or `metrics: { exportInterval: "60 seconds" }`. Previous flat exporter options must move under their signal. Set `traces: false` to disable browser tracing and exception capture. Disabling logs does not affect browser exception capture: global errors and unhandled promise rejections are exported as always-sampled failed spans. The base URL defaults to `/api/otel`.

The layer starts when the API runtime atom is read. To start global exception capture during hydration, read it once in the root component:

```tsx
useAtomValue(ApiClient.runtime);
```

## Browser Lifecycle

Pending telemetry is flushed when the document becomes hidden and on `pagehide`, using fetch `keepalive` for lifecycle requests. Listeners and flush fibers are scoped to the browser layer. Delivery remains best-effort: browsers impose an approximately 64 KiB aggregate keepalive body budget, and abrupt process termination or offline navigation can still lose telemetry. Routine exports are not forced to use keepalive. Visible-only visibility changes do not flush, and empty trace buffers do not generate requests.

## Server Environment Variables

- `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` - server service name
- `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` - shared server and browser-proxy OTLP collector base URL
- `OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` - enables server log export and the browser log proxy route
- `OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=otlp` - enables server metric export and the browser metric proxy route
- `OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=otlp` - enables server trace export and the browser trace proxy route
- `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES` - optional server resource attributes
